\import{fcap-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}

\tag{fcap}
\tag{spin}
\tag{quadratic-forms}

\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Changing compact-open reference subgroups}

\p{Let #{Q} be nondegenerate on a finite-dimensional rational space
#{V}. The \vocabk{compatible compact-open reference tuples}{fcap-004C}
constructed from any two rational bases in \ref{fcap-004D} agree at
all but finitely many primes, in each of their O, SO and Spin families.}

\p{There is also a finite shrinking operation. Given any compatible
tuple #{U}, choose open subgroups #{W_p\leq O_p} and a finite set
#{F} such that #{U_p^O\subseteq W_p} for #{p\notin F}. Then

##{\begin{gathered}
\widetilde U_p^O=U_p^O\cap W_p,\\
\widetilde U_p^{\Spin}
=U_p^{\Spin}\cap(i_p\rho_p)^{-1}(W_p),\\
\widetilde U_p^{SO}=i_p^{-1}(\widetilde U_p^O)
\end{gathered}}

is another compatible tuple, contained in #{U} at every prime and
equal to it outside #{F}. In particular one may take #{W_p=O_p}
outside a chosen finite set. The maps #{i_p} and #{\rho_p} are those
of \ref{fcap-004C}.}

\subtree{
\taxon{Proof}
\p{For bases #{b} and #{b'}, let #{C} express the vectors of #{b'}
in the basis #{b}. Let #{F_b} contain the denominator primes of
every entry of both #{C} and #{C^{-1}}. This is a finite set.
Write #{L_p,L'_p} for the respective #{\mathbb Z_p}-spans of
#{b_p,b'_p}. For #{p\notin F_b}, the entries of #{C} belong to
#{\mathbb Z_p}, so every vector of #{b'_p} belongs to #{L_p}.
Applying #{C^{-1}} gives the reverse containment. Thus
#{L'_p=L_p}. Their stabilizers in #{O_p} are equal. Taking full
preimages under #{i_p\rho_p} and #{i_p} gives equality of the Spin
and SO references as well. No integrality condition on the
coefficients of #{Q} is needed.}

\p{For shrinking, an open subgroup of a topological group is closed:
its complement is the union of its other open cosets. Hence
#{U_p^O\cap W_p} is open and is a closed subset of the compact
group #{U_p^O}. The continuous action #{i_p\rho_p} has an open and
closed preimage of #{W_p}. Intersecting this preimage with the
compact-open #{U_p^{\Spin}} likewise gives an open compact
subgroup. The action sends the Spin intersection into the orthogonal
intersection. The derived SO group has the required properties by
\ref{fcap-004C}.}

\p{If #{p\notin F}, the orthogonal intersection equals #{U_p^O}.
Compatibility of #{U} gives
#{i_p\rho_p(U_p^{\Spin})\subseteq U_p^O\subseteq W_p}, so the
Spin intersection also equals #{U_p^{\Spin}}; the derived SO
preimages are then equal. For a rational point #{x}, adjoining
#{F} to its original finite exceptional set proves eventual
membership in the new references. The union is taken separately
for each #{x}, as required. In dimension zero all groups are
singletons and these statements retain their literal meanings.}
}

\p{For a concrete basis change in positive dimension, replace
#{b_1} by #{2b_1} and leave the other basis vectors fixed. The two
change matrices have diagonal entries #{2} and #{1/2}, respectively,
with all other diagonal entries #{1}. Thus #{F_b=\{2\}} suffices.
The two lattices agree at every odd prime, while at #{2} the new
lattice is strictly smaller. This does not assert that their
orthogonal stabilizers must differ at #{2}.}

\p{These are deductions from the lattice construction in
\ref{fcap-004D} and the continuous local action, using the
coordinate topology of \citet{Proposition 2.1, p. 2}{conrad2011adelic}.
The basis comparison is formalized by
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.QuadraticMap.eventually_integralOrthogonalSubgroup_baseChange_eq},
which also permits degenerate forms. The compatible shrinking
construction is
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.QuadraticMap.OrthogonalCompactOpens.infOpenSubgroup}.
Neither construction requires a lift of an arbitrary integral
orthogonal point to Spin.}
